                    <run>Æ&#10;</run>
                    <run fontcolor='#000000'>- Estimations associated with the RDP and CKT page have a single estimation endpoint.</run>
                    <run>Æ&#10;&#10;</run>
                    <run fontcolor='#000000' underline='true'>Therefore, the mapping is done as follows:</run>
                    <run>Æ&#10;</run>
                    <run fontcolor='#000000'>- Estimations with a multiple estimation endpoint are estimations that belong to the RLP.</run>
                    <run>Æ&#10;</run>
                    <run fontcolor='#000000'>- We do not have a systematic way to identify if an estimation is associated with the RDP or CKT page, since they all appear as single endpoint estimations. But we are able to deduce it analytically:</run>
                    <run>Æ&#10;</run>
                    <run fontcolor='#000000'>    -  The first estimation with a single endpoint for a session is assumed to be  for the RDP page, since the user still hasn’t had the chance to continue to the CKT page.</run>
                    <run>Æ&#10;</run>
                    <run fontcolor='#000000'>    -  Similarly, if there is a transaction associated with the session, we can deduce that the last estimation with a single endpoint must be of the CKT page.</run>
                    <run>Æ&#10;</run>
                    <run fontcolor='#000000'>    -  A transaction has to be associated with the session, otherwise we cannot be sure that the estimation came from the CKT page and not from the Restaurant Details page.</run>
                    <run>Æ&#10;&#10;</run>
                    <run fontcolor='#000000' underline='true'>More validation:</run>
                    <run>Æ&#10;</run>
                    <run fontcolor='#000000'>Due to general data quality issues we cannot rule out that some estimations do not get recorded. Therefore:</run>
                    <run>Æ&#10;</run>
                    <run fontcolor='#000000'>- RDP estimations must be the first single endpoint estimation where the basket is empty.</run>
                    <run>Æ&#10;</run>
                    <run fontcolor='#000000'>- CKT estimations must be the last multiple endpoint estimation with a not-empty shopping basket.</run>
                    <run>Æ&#10;&#10;</run>
                    <run fontcolor='#000000' underline='true'>Known Issue:</run>
                    <run>Æ&#10;</run>
                    <run fontcolor='#000000'>- There is no validation that the CKT estimation happens before the transaction. Theoretically, if the user made an order and landed later on the CKT page, the second landing will be counted as the CKT estimation. The impact of this issue is probably small.</run>
                    <run>Æ&#10;&#10;</run>
                    <run bold='true' fontcolor='#000000' fontsize='11'>What estimations are filtered out?</run>
                    <run>Æ&#10;</run>
                    <run fontcolor='#000000'>- We cannot curate all the estimations, since we have too many of them.</run>
                    <run>Æ&#10;</run>
                    <run fontcolor='#000000'>    - This is true especially RLP estimations, since we get an estimation for every vendor the user had in the list.</run>
                    <run>Æ&#10;</run>
                    <run fontcolor='#000000'>- We filter out estimations we consider irrelevant, in order to keep the runtimes and table sizes reasonable.</run>
                    <run>Æ&#10;</run>
                    <run fontcolor='#000000'>- Estimations that cannot be part of any “delta” calculation are considered irrelevant.</run>
                    <run>Æ&#10;</run>
                    <run fontcolor='#000000'>     - For example, an RLP estimation without a matching RDP estimation is irrelevant since we cannot compute any pages delta jumps with this information.</run>
                    <run>Æ&#10;</run>
                    <run fontcolor='#000000'>- We match between the different estimations - an estimation of a certain page “matches” an estimation in a different page if:</run>
                    <run>Æ&#10;</run>
                    <run fontcolor='#000000'>    - It belongs to the same session and the same vendor</run>
                    <run>Æ&#10;</run>
                    <run fontcolor='#000000'>    - It arrived in the right chronological order (RLP estimation must have an earlier timestamp than RDP; RDP estimation must have an earlier timestamp than CKT).</run>
                    <run>Æ&#10;&#10;</run>
                    <run fontcolor='#000000' fontsize='10' underline='true'>RDP:</run>
                    <run>Æ&#10;</run>
                    <run fontcolor='#000000'>- Only RDP estimations with a matching CKT or a matching RLP enter the dashboard.</run>
                    <run>Æ&#10;&#10;</run>
                    <run fontcolor='#000000' fontsize='10' underline='true'>RLP:</run>
                    <run>Æ&#10;</run>
                    <run fontcolor='#000000'>- Only RLP estimations with a matching RDP enter the dashboard.</run>
                    <run>Æ&#10;</run>
                    <run fontcolor='#000000'>    - In other words, the user has to later enter the RDP of the vendor for the RLP estimation to appear.</run>
                    <run>Æ&#10;&#10;</run>
                    <run fontcolor='#000000' fontsize='10' underline='true'>CKT:</run>
                    <run>Æ&#10;</run>
                    <run fontcolor='#000000'>- Only CKT estimations with a matching RDP enter the dashboard.</run>
                    <run>Æ&#10;</run>
                    <run fontcolor='#000000'>- Only sessions with associated transactions have an CKT estimation associated with them.</run>
                    <run>Æ&#10;</run>
                    <run fontcolor='#000000'>    - If there is no transaction associated with the session, we can’t know that a single endpoint estimation is coming from the CKT page, even if it is the last one.</run>
                    <run>Æ&#10;&#10;</run>
                    <run bold='true' fontcolor='#000000' fontsize='11'>How do we handle the components?</run>
                    <run>Æ&#10;</run>
                    <run fontcolor='#000000'>- The components arriving from the TES service can be positive or negative, whole number or fraction. Mostly, they are positive and whole.</run>
                    <run>Æ&#10;</run>
                    <run fontcolor='#000000'>- The PDT is calculated as the sum of the components that participate in the PDT calculation, however fractions are rounded down (18.8 is calculated as 18) and negative values are calculated as 0 for the overall PDT.</run>
                    <run>Æ&#10;</run>
                    <run fontcolor='#000000'>- We convert negative values we receive from TES to 0. This prevents a situation where the average PDT seems too far away from the sum of components.</run>
                    <run>Æ&#10;</run>
                    <run fontcolor='#000000'>- Nevertheless, as of now, we do not round down fractions.</run>
                    <run>Æ&#10;&#10;</run>
                    <run bold='true' fontcolor='#000000' fontsize='11'>Why do bug fixes and other changes only affect data coming into the dashboard after the implementation?</run>
                    <run>Æ&#10;</run>
                    <run fontcolor='#000000'>- Due to the amount and complexity of the computations needed to create the dashboard, the runtime is very long.</run>
                    <run>Æ&#10;</run>
                    <run fontcolor='#000000'>- Daily runs compute the data for 2 days at a time and the time it takes is reasonable.</run>
                    <run>Æ&#10;</run>
                    <run fontcolor='#000000'>- Backfilling data requires computing the data for months at a time. This would block the production environment for (at least) days and block all the other tasks.</run>
                    <run>Æ&#10;&#10;</run>
                    <run bold='true' fontcolor='#000000' fontsize='11'>What is the absolute value filter?</run>
                    <run>Æ&#10;</run>
                    <run fontcolor='#000000'>- The absolute value of a number is its distance from the zero.</run>
                    <run>Æ&#10;</run>
                    <run fontcolor='#000000'>    - The absolute value of -2 is +2. The absolute value of 0 is 0. The absolute value of +5 is +5.</run>
                    <run>Æ&#10;</run>
                    <run fontcolor='#000000'>- When the delta is not shown in absolute value, the averages of the delta are shown.</run>
                    <run>Æ&#10;</run>
                    <run fontcolor='#000000'>    - Imagine we have 2 sessions and therefore two RDP-RLP deltas: -2 and +3. The average delta would be 0.5.</run>
                    <run>Æ&#10;</run>
                    <run fontcolor='#000000'>- When the delta is shown in absolute value, the deltas are first converted to their absolute values, then the average delta is calculated.</run>
                    <run>Æ&#10;</run>
                    <run fontcolor='#000000'>    - Imagine we have 2 sessions and therefore two RDP-RLP deltas: -2 and +3. The absolute values are +2 and +3. The average delta is +2.5.</run>
                    <run>Æ&#10;&#10;</run>
                    <run bold='true' fontcolor='#000000'>- Delta in non-absolute value measures Bias.</run>
                    <run>Æ&#10;</run>
                    <run bold='true' fontcolor='#000000'>- Delta in absolute-value measures the Error.</run>
                  </formatted-text>
                  <zone-style>
                    <format attr='border-color' value='#000000' />
                    <format attr='border-style' value='none' />
                    <format attr='border-width' value='0' />
                    <format attr='margin' value='4' />
